  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-79391",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3 & =13 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
